      "content": "<h3>What is a HashMap?</h3>\n<p>A <strong>HashMap</strong> stores <strong>pairs</strong>: a <strong>key</strong> and the <strong>value</strong> that belongs to it. You give the map a key and it gives back the value almost instantly, like looking up a city in a telephone-code table: key <code>\"Pune\"</code>, value <code>20</code>. Each key appears <strong>once</strong>; each key maps to one value.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Package <code>java.util</code>. Implements <code>Map&lt;K, V&gt;</code>. It is <strong>not</strong> a <code>Collection</code> (it has its own interface).</li>\n<li><strong>Unique keys:</strong> putting an existing key again <strong>replaces the value</strong>. Values may repeat.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast:</strong> <code>get</code>, <code>put</code>, <code>remove</code> and <code>containsKey</code> are O(1) on average.</li>\n<li><strong>No order:</strong> iteration order depends on hash codes and can change when the map grows. Use <code>LinkedHashMap</code> or <code>TreeMap</code> for an order.</li>\n<li><strong>One null key</strong> and any number of <strong>null values</strong> are allowed.</li>\n<li><strong>Not thread-safe.</strong> For threads use <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code>.</li>\n<li>Key classes must have correct <code>equals()</code> and <code>hashCode()</code> (String, Integer and records already do).</li>\n</ul>"
